// A composite built once per loop iteration and handed to the host.
//
// The counterpart to `12_sensor_window.kel`. The construction site is
// the same shape -- one `Reading` per iteration of a bounded `for` --
// but the value LEAVES the iteration through `yield`, so the host may
// still be holding it when the next iteration builds its successor.
//
// That difference is the whole subject of the composite-region-reuse
// work: the confined site may share one slot across iterations, and
// this one may not. A planner that reused a slot here would hand the
// host the next reading's bytes where it asked for this one's, and
// the arena's epoch guard would NOT catch it, because an overwrite in
// place advances no epoch.
//
// This is also the first script in this directory to use `loop main`
// at all, so it is the only one exercising `Stream`, `Yield` and the
// `Reset` at a stream-cycle boundary.
//
// A note on the closing yield. A `loop` body must yield on EVERY path
// from `Stream` to `Reset`, and the path that skips a bounded `for`
// entirely does not, so yielding only inside the loop is refused as a
// productivity violation. The end-of-cycle marker below is what makes
// the stream productive, and it is the conservative-verification
// stance doing its job rather than a workaround.
//
// NOT RUNNABLE THROUGH `keleusma run`, and that is a property of the
// command rather than of this script. The command refuses a `loop main`
// whose yields are not `Word`:
//
//   error: loop main yielded a non-Word value (signature requires Word)
//
// Yielding a COMPOSITE is the entire point here, so the script is driven
// from a host instead. `tests/corpus_pattern_coverage.rs` compiles it,
// and any embedder can step it with `call_with_shared` / `resume_with_shared`.
//
// Per cycle it yields Reading{0,1}, Reading{1,8}, Reading{2,15}, then the
// end marker Reading{9,0}, then resets. Observed on the virtual machine,
// the four composites occupy four DISTINCT addresses within one epoch,
// and the addresses repeat at the next epoch after the reset.

struct Reading { channel: Word, value: Word }

// Stand-in for a host sensor read, kept pure so the script stays total.
fn sample(channel: Word) -> Word {
    channel * 7 + 1
}

loop main(ack: Word) -> Reading {
    let channels = [0, 1, 2];

    for c in channels {
        // Built here, observed by the host, and still reachable from the
        // host when the next iteration runs.
        let _ack = yield Reading { channel: c, value: sample(c) };
    }

    // The end-of-cycle marker, yielded on every path so the stream is
    // productive whatever the channel list contains.
    let _end = yield Reading { channel: 9, value: 0 };

    // The cycle's closing value. `loop` bodies discard it and reset.
    Reading { channel: 9, value: 0 }
}
